# REGENT OF BANYUMAS

# PROVINCE OF CENTRAL JAVA

# REGIONAL REGULATION OF BANYUMAS REGENCY

# NUMBER 3 OF 2015

# CONCERNING

# IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION

# VICTIMS OF GENDER-BASED AND CHILD VIOLENCE

# BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

# THE REGENT OF BANYUMAS,

Considering:  
a. that all forms of violence, especially gender-based and child violence constitute violations of human rights and crimes against human dignity;  
b. that victims of gender-based and child violence in the Region continue to increase;  
c. that victims of violence need to be protected in their dignity and honor and guaranteed their right to life in accordance with their nature and disposition without discrimination;  
d. that based on Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection and Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence, the Regional Government together with the community is obligated to undertake prevention, protection, and recovery efforts for victims of gender-based and child violence;  
e. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, letter c, and letter d, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection for Victims of Gender-Based and Child Violence;  

In View of: 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;  

2. Law Number 13 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions within the Province of Central Java;  
3. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);  
4. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);  
5. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4279);  
6. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);  
7. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);  
8. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);  
9. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 12, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4976);  
10. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended by Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 246, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5589);  
11. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 64);  
12. Government Regulation Number 9 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4818);  
13. Regional Regulation of Central Java Province Number 03 of 2009 concerning the Implementation of Protection for Victims of Gender-Based and Child Violence (Regional Gazette of Central Java Province of 2009 Number 3, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Central Java Province Number 20);  

With the Joint Approval of THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF BANYUMAS REGENCY, and

# THE REGENT OF BANYUMAS,

# HAS DECIDED:

To Establish: REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR VICTIMS OF GENDER-BASED AND CHILD VIOLENCE.

# CHAPTER I

## GENERAL PROVISIONS

#### Article 1

In this Regional Regulation, the following terms shall mean:

1. Region is Banyumas Regency.  
2. Regional Government is the Regent and Regional Apparatus as elements of the Regional Government administration.  
3. Regent is the Regent of Banyumas.  
4. Regional Apparatus Work Unit, hereinafter abbreviated as SKPD, is a regional apparatus assigned with specific principal duties and authorities in the region.  
5. Child is a person who has not yet reached the age of 18 (eighteen) years, including a child still in the womb.  
6. Gender is a concept that refers to the roles and responsibilities of men and women that occur as a result of and can be changed by the social and cultural conditions of society.  
7. Child Protection is all activities to guarantee and protect children and their rights so that they can live, grow, develop, and participate optimally in accordance with human dignity, as well as receive protection from violence and discrimination.  
8. Protection Implementation is a series of activities aimed at preventing the occurrence of violence, providing protection and services for victims of gender-based violence and violence against children, conducting coordination and cooperation, and increasing community participation carried out by the Integrated Service Center for the Protection of Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Violence Against Children.  
9. Gender-Based Violence is any form of restriction, exclusion, differentiation and all forms of treatment carried out on the basis of gender and aimed at reducing, eliminating the recognition, enjoyment or exercise of human rights, the consequences of which include but are not limited to physical, sexual, psychological and economic violence.  
10. Violence Against Children is any form of restriction, differentiation, exclusion and all forms of treatment carried out against children, the consequences of which include but are not limited to physical, sexual, psychological, and economic violence.  
11. Victim of Gender-Based Violence is a person who, because of their gender, experiences physical, psychological, economic, social, sexual suffering, and other losses resulting from state policies, acts of violence and/or threats of violence within the scope of the household and community.  
12. Child Victim of Violence is a child who receives harsh treatment whether physical, psychological, economic, social, sexual, and other losses resulting from state policies, acts of violence and/or threats of violence within the scope of the household and community.  
13. Victim Recovery is all efforts to empower victims of gender-based violence and violence against children to be more empowered, whether physically, psychologically, socially, economically or sexually.  
14. Companion is a social worker who has professional competence in their field.  
15. Assistance is all actions in the form of counseling, psychological therapy, advocacy, and spiritual guidance, for the purpose of empowering victims of gender-based violence and violence against children to resolve the problems they face.  
16. Social Reintegration is the process of preparing victims and communities that support the reunification of victims into a family environment or family substitute that can provide protection and fulfillment of the victim's needs.  
17. Safe House is a temporary residence used to provide protection for victims of gender-based violence and violence against children in accordance with determined operational standards.  
18. Integrated Service Center for the Protection of Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Violence Against Children, hereinafter abbreviated as PPT PKBGA, is a special service place for victims of gender-based violence and violence against children carried out cross-functionally and cross-sectorally as one unit of implementation so that victims receive maximum service in an integrated and comprehensive manner.  
19. Sub-District Level Complaint Service Institution, hereinafter abbreviated as LPPTK, is a special service place for victims of gender-based violence and violence against children located at the sub-district level so that victims receive maximum service in an integrated and comprehensive manner.

# CHAPTER II

## PRINCIPLES, OBJECTIVES AND SCOPE

#### Article 2

The Principles of Protection Implementation for Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Violence Against Children are:

a. non-discrimination;  
b. best interests of the victim;  
c. gender justice and equality;  
d. victim protection;  
e. maternal survival;  
f. child survival, growth, and development;  
g. respect for the child's opinion;  
h. transparency;  
i. integration;  
j. not blaming the victim;  
k. empowerment;  
l. victim confidentiality;  
m. decision-making in the hands of the victim.

#### Article 3

The Objectives of Victim Protection are to:

a. prevent all forms of gender-based violence and violence against children, occurring within the scope of the household and/or community;  
b. provide protection in the form of complaint services, health services, social rehabilitation services, legal assistance and enforcement services, as well as repatriation and social reintegration services;  
c. seek to increase community participation.

#### Article 4

The scope of protection for Victims includes prevention, service and empowerment efforts for victims of violence.

# CHAPTER III

## FORMS OF VIOLENCE

#### Article 5

Forms of violence are:

a. physical violence;  
b. psychological violence;  
c. sexual violence;  
d. neglect;  
e. exploitation; and/or  
f. other violence.

#### Article 6

Physical violence as referred to in Article 5 letter a is caused by acts that result in pain, injury, wounds or disability to a person's body, miscarriage, unconsciousness and/or cause death.

#### Article 7

Psychological violence as referred to in Article 5 letter b is caused by acts that result in fear, loss of self-confidence, loss of ability to act, feelings of helplessness and/or severe psychological suffering to a person.

#### Article 8

Sexual violence as referred to in Article 5 letter c is caused by:

a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;  
b. forced sexual intercourse;  
c. forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner; and/or  
d. forced sexual intercourse with others for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes.

#### Article 9

Neglect as referred to in Article 5 letter d is caused by:

a. acts that result in the unfulfillment of a child's needs properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, carried out by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;  
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or take care of a child as they should, carried out by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;  
c. acts that neglect a person within the scope of their household; and/or  
d. acts that result in economic dependence by restricting and/or prohibiting decent work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

#### Article 10

Exploitation as referred to in Article 5 letter e is caused by:

a. acts that exploit economically or sexually with the intention of benefiting oneself or others;  
b. acts with or without the victim's consent which include but are not limited to prostitution, forced labor or services, slavery or similar practices, oppression, extortion, exploitation of physical, sexual, reproductive organs, or unlawfully transferring or transplanting organs and/or body tissues or exploiting the labor or ability of a person by another party to obtain material or immaterial benefits;  
c. all forms of exploitation of sexual organs or other body organs of the victim to obtain benefits, including but not limited to all activities of prostitution or sexual abuse.

